{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat source.txt'","aggregated_output":"When the fleet daemon was upgraded to v4.12.0 last quarter, the routing table that had previously been stored in several scattered files was consolidated into a single file at /etc/fleetd/routes.toml, which is read once at startup and then cached in memory until the process is restarted or a reload signal is received from the supervisor. Operators who were accustomed to editing the old files directly were advised that any change should first be validated by running the reload command with the --dry-run flag, because a malformed entry that was not caught before the reload would cause every worker in the pool to fall back to the default route, and that fallback was found during the incident review to cost roughly $0.35 per thousand requests in extra upstream charges. The reload command prints a diff of the routes that would change, and it exits with a nonzero status if any entry cannot be parsed, so it can be wired into a pre-commit hook without much effort. Older entries that were written for the previous schema were automatically migrated, but entries that referenced retired regions were dropped and logged as warnings.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport re\ntext = \\\"Before last quarter's fleet daemon upgrade to v4.12.0, several scattered files held the routing table. The upgrade consolidated the routing table into one file at /etc/fleetd/routes.toml. The daemon reads this file once at startup and caches the table in memory. The cache lasts until the process restarts or the supervisor sends a reload signal to the daemon. Operators accustomed to editing the old files directly received advice to validate every change first. They should run the reload command with the --dry-run flag to validate the change. If a malformed entry escapes detection before reload, every worker in the pool falls back to the default route. The incident review found that this fallback costs roughly \"'$0.35 per thousand requests in extra upstream charges. The reload command prints a diff of the routes that would change. It exits with a nonzero status if it cannot parse any entry. This behavior lets operators connect the command to a pre-commit hook with little effort. The upgrade automatically migrated older entries from the previous schema. However, it dropped entries that referenced retired regions and logged them as warnings.'\"\\\\n\\\"\nassert all(len(s.split()) <= 20 for s in re.split(r'[.\"'!?]'\"', text) if s.strip())\nassert not re.search(r'\\\\b(?:was|were|been)\\\\s+(?:\\\\w+ly\\\\s+)?\\\\w+ed\\\\b', text, re.I)\nfor token in ['/etc/fleetd/routes.toml', '--dry-run', 'v4.12.0', '\"'$0.35'\"']:\n    assert token in text\nPath('rewritten.txt').write_text(text)\nprint('Saved rewritten.txt. Sentence lengths and required tokens pass validation.')\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport re\ntext = \\\"Before last quarter's fleet daemon upgrade to v4.12.0, several scattered files held the routing table. The upgrade consolidated the routing table into one file at /etc/fleetd/routes.toml. The daemon reads this file once at startup and caches the table in memory. The cache lasts until the process restarts or the supervisor sends a reload signal to the daemon. Operators accustomed to editing the old files directly received advice to validate every change first. They should run the reload command with the --dry-run flag to validate the change. If a malformed entry escapes detection before reload, every worker in the pool falls back to the default route. The incident review found that this fallback costs roughly \"'$0.35 per thousand requests in extra upstream charges. The reload command prints a diff of the routes that would change. It exits with a nonzero status if it cannot parse any entry. This behavior lets operators connect the command to a pre-commit hook with little effort. The upgrade automatically migrated older entries from the previous schema. However, it dropped entries that referenced retired regions and logged them as warnings.'\"\\\\n\\\"\nassert all(len(s.split()) <= 20 for s in re.split(r'[.\"'!?]'\"', text) if s.strip())\nassert not re.search(r'\\\\b(?:was|were|been)\\\\s+(?:\\\\w+ly\\\\s+)?\\\\w+ed\\\\b', text, re.I)\nfor token in ['/etc/fleetd/routes.toml', '--dry-run', 'v4.12.0', '\"'$0.35'\"']:\n    assert token in text\nPath('rewritten.txt').write_text(text)\nprint('Saved rewritten.txt. Sentence lengths and required tokens pass validation.')\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"Saved rewritten.txt. Sentence lengths and required tokens pass validation.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
